## Ripcord: raw packet captures. These can contain sensitive material (PSN auth tokens,
## session/registration keys, account identifiers) and must never be committed as-is - only
## the factual wire-format spec docs written from them belong in the repo. See the plan's
## clean-room methodology and docs/protocol-research-log.md.
docs/protocol/captures/

## Ripcord: captures by TYPE as well as by location. The rule above is a directory, and for eleven
## rounds that was the whole of it - a capture saved anywhere else was not ignored at all. These files
## definitionally hold raw session material: OAuth access and refresh tokens, authorization codes,
## WebAuthn material, account identifiers, date of birth, console names, device ids and candidate IP
## addresses. Nothing in this repository survives one of them being committed by accident, so the rule
## must not depend on remembering which directory to save into.
##
## The compressed forms matter more than the bare ones: five of the ten capture filenames in
## docs/protocol-research-log.md end .pcapng.gz, so gzip is this project's actual practice and the
## by-type rule missed all of it for a round. Nothing legitimate here is a .gz or .zip; if that ever
## changes, force-add the one file rather than narrowing these.
*.pcap
*.pcapng
*.cap
*.saz
*.har
*.etl
*.utrace
*.gz
*.zip

## Ripcord: docs/ was withheld in full pending a publication review. That review happened on
## 2026-09-06 and docs/ is now committed and ships - EXCEPT the dirty room, which is withheld by
## the rule above. ("Ships" means it is in the tree a clone receives. The GitHub repository itself
## is still private pending a final review; nothing here depends on that, but do not read these
## comments as a statement about visibility.) That rule was always kept separate for exactly this moment, and un-ignoring docs/
## is precisely why it must not be collapsed into this one. The dirty room stays ignored.

## Ripcord: the OAuth client credential and the signed-in account's tokens. These are the
## runtime files HalyardClientConfigFile and AccountTokenStore read, and they normally live
## in the per-user config directory rather than the repo - these rules exist for the case
## where someone drops one in the tree while developing. client.json holds an application
## secret; account.json holds a refresh token, which is a durable credential for a real
## person's PSN account. Neither is ever committed. Note that this is NOT the same thing as
## the bundled application credential in Ripcord.Cloud.Halyard/Data/, which is committed
## deliberately and argued in NOTICE, "Application OAuth credential" - that one is generic to
## the application, while these two are per-user and per-machine. An earlier revision of this
## comment claimed "no credential is compiled in", which the bundle has made untrue; the
## distinction that actually holds is application versus user, not compiled-in versus not.
client.json
account.json

## Ripcord: per-machine Claude Code state. Local tool-permission grants, of use to nobody
## else, and they record the paths this machine happened to read - including comparison reads
## of other implementations, which are a permitted audit but have no business in a public
## tree whose central claim is independent derivation. Findings belong in the dirty room.
.claude/

## Ripcord: root-level project prose was withheld pending the same publication review, and now
## ships - README, CONTRIBUTING, SECURITY, CODE_OF_CONDUCT, ROADMAP and CLAUDE.md are all in the
## tree. Nothing root-anchored is withheld any more. .claude/ (below) is a different thing and
## stays ignored: it is per-machine tool state, not project prose.

## Ripcord: build output for the ports/ tree, which is Makefile-driven and so lands nowhere
## the [Bb]in/ and [Oo]bj/ rules below would catch it. Scoped to ports/ rather than a bare
## build/ so it cannot quietly swallow a directory somebody adds elsewhere.
ports/*/build/
ports/*/tests/build/
*.3dsx
*.vpk
*.smdh
*.cia
*.elf

## Ripcord: the ports' generated interoperability constants. ports/ripcord-3ds does not keep
## a copy of src/Ripcord.Protocol.Halyard/Data/halyard-v1-constants.json - it generates a C
## translation unit from that one committed file at build time. This rule is what keeps that
## true: a second checked-in copy of those bytes would turn one carefully bounded NOTICE
## exception into two, and the second would carry no argument at all. The generated file
## normally lands under build/ and is already covered above; this is the belt-and-braces rule
## for the case where somebody points the generator somewhere else.
*halyard_v1_constants.g.c
*halyard_v1_constants.g.h

## Ripcord: known-answer vectors emitted by `ProtocolLab vectors` for the ports to check
## themselves against. Derived from the committed bundle plus synthetic inputs, so they carry
## nothing sensitive - but they are a build artifact with a one-command recipe, and a
## committed copy would only invite the question of whether it is a second bundle.
ports/*/tests/vectors/

